entries.body:
The term "halving" refers to a scheduled event that cuts in half the reward granted to miners for each block mined via the block subsidy. This reduction applies specifically to the part of the block subsidy made up of new bitcoins created ex nihilo. The halving was designed by Satoshi Nakamoto, the creator of Bitcoin, as a mechanism to control inflation and ensure a limited supply of bitcoins.

The initial block subsidy was 50 bitcoins, and the halving occurs every 210,000 blocks mined, which takes approximately four years. The halvings have taken place:
* At block 210,000 on November 28, 2012, reducing the subsidy to 25 BTC;
* At block 420,000 on July 9, 2016, reducing the subsidy to 12.5 BTC;
* At block 630,000 on May 11, 2020, reducing the subsidy to 6.25 BTC;
* At block 840,000 on April 20, 2024, reducing the subsidy to 3.125 BTC.

Halvings will continue to occur until the block subsidy reaches zero, at which point the maximum supply of approximately 21 million bitcoins will have been reached. The next Bitcoin halving is expected to take place around spring 2028, although the exact date may vary slightly depending on block mining time. At that point, the block subsidy will be reduced from 3.125 to 1.5625 bitcoins. This will be the fifth halving in Bitcoin's history. As block subsidies decrease, transaction fees become an increasingly important source of revenue for miners, which ensures their motivation to continue participating in proof of work.

A proof-of-work system designed by Adam Back in 1997 to fight spam and DoS attacks. It is based on the principle that a sender must perform computational work (specifically, searching for a partial collision on a cryptographic hash function) to prove their work. This task is costly in time and energy for the sender, but verification of the result by the recipient is quick and simple. This protocol proved particularly well suited to fighting spam in email systems, because it places little burden on legitimate users, while constituting a major obstacle for spammers. Indeed, sending a single email requires a few seconds of computation, but repeating this operation millions of times makes it extremely costly in terms of energy and time, which often cancels out the economic appeal of spam campaigns, whether for marketing or malicious purposes. Moreover, it preserves the anonymity of the sender. 

HashCash was quickly adopted by cypherpunks who were seeking to develop an anonymous electronic cash system without an intermediary. Indeed, Adam Back's innovation introduced for the first time the notion of scarcity in the digital world. The concept of proof of work is then found in several electronic money systems predating Bitcoin, including:
* b-money by Wei Dai, published in 1998;
* RPOW by Hal Finney, published in 2004;
* Bit Gold by Nick Szabo, published in 2005.

The principle of HashCash is also found within the Bitcoin protocol, where it is used as a protection mechanism against Sybil attacks.

Acronym for “*Hashed Timelock Contract*”. It is a smart contract mechanism mainly used on Lightning. It is also sometimes found in *atomic swaps*. Fundamentally, the HTLC makes it possible to make a transfer of funds conditional on the revelation of a secret, and also includes a time condition to protect the sender's money in case the transaction fails.

On Lightning, HTLCs make it possible to send bitcoins to a node with which one does not have a direct channel, by passing through several channels, without needing trust along the route. The payment between each node is conditional on the provision of a preimage (the secret which, when hashed, matches an agreed value). If the final recipient provides this preimage, they can claim the funds, and necessarily allow each intermediate node to also claim the funds in cascade. 

For example, if Alice wants to send 1 BTC to David, but she does not have a direct channel with him, she must go through Bob and Carol, who have payment channels between them. Here is how the transaction unfolds:
* David presents a Lightning *invoice* to Alice. It contains the hash `h` of a secret `s` (the preimage) that only David knows. We therefore have: `h\ =\ hachage(s)`;
* Alice creates an HTLC with Bob, offering to send him 1 BTC on condition that Bob provides her with a secret `s` (the preimage) that matches the hash `h`;
* Bob creates an HTLC with Carol, offering to send her 1 BTC on condition that she provides the same secret `s`;
* Carol creates an HTLC with David, offering 1 BTC again if he reveals the preimage `s`;
* David reveals `s` (which he was the only one to know) to Carol to receive 1 BTC. Carol can now use `s` to recover the BTC from Bob. And Bob, who now knows `s`, does the same with Alice.

Ultimately, Alice sent 1 BTC to David via Bob and Carol (a neutral action for the latter), without anyone having to trust each other, because everything is secured in cascade by the conditions of the HTLCs.

HTLCs thus make it possible to carry out so-called “atomic” exchanges: either the transfer is entirely successful, or it fails, and the funds are returned. This guarantees the security of transactions even between several participants without needing trust.
